bout 36ms of the 75ms total and stays constant regardless of APC count. Moving that NTT computation to GPU could help, though a previous attempt with multi-streaming failed due to VPMM issues, so I'd need a different approach that avoids that problem.\n\nEven if I could save 25-30ms by moving NTT to GPU, the savings would be similar at both APC 0 and APC 300, so it wouldn't actually improve scaling. The real opportunity is in MLE Rounds, where APC 300 is 43ms slower than APC 0 because of 6.3x more traces requiring per-trace processing. I could reduce that overhead by amortizing the trace categorization and TraceCtx construction—right now each MLE round iterates over all traces to categorize them and build contexts separately, which is wasteful.\n\nThe math shows that pre-computing categorization across all 14 rounds would save maybe 8ms out of the 9ms total CPU overhead, which is marginal. But I'm realizing that TraceCtx caching was already partially tackled in an earlier optimization task, so I should check what was actually done there before proposing more work.\n\nLooking at the actual bottleneck differently: the stacked matrix width is what really matters for hashing cost. At APC 300, we're hashing 106K-column rows with ~13,250 Poseidon2 permutations each, whereas at APC 0 it's only 3,919 columns requiring ~490 permutations. The height trade-off is the opposite—fewer rows at APC 300 but more at APC 0—so I need to figure out which dimension actually dominates the total hash cost.\n\nLet me work through the numbers: APC 300 ends up with roughly 15K rows across 2 segments, while APC 0 has around 972K rows spread across 5 segments. Multiplying through the permutation counts, APC 0 is actually doing significantly more total Poseidon2 work despite the narrower columns, which suggests the height is the real problem here.\n\nBut the actual timing shows only a 2.4x difference when the math predicts 5.9x, which points to a GPU efficiency issue. With fewer columns per row at APC 0, each thread gets less work to do, so the GPU isn't being fully utilized even though there's more total computation happening.\n\nAt APC 300, the GPU is saturated with compute work per thread, so the kernel is already running near peak throughput. That means optimizing Trace Commit won't move the needle on the APC 300 vs APC 0 gap. The real opportunity is elsewhere — specifically in the Merkle tree construction within WHIR at APC 300, which takes 128ms.\n\nLooking at the WHIR flow, the Merkle tree construction for the folded polynomial is eating up roughly 80ms (about 63% of WHIR time), so there's potential to save 20-40ms there. But the tree operates on a different matrix than Trace Commit, so I can't reuse that work. After exploring this angle, I think I've found the most promising optimization path and should move forward with writing it up.\n\nNow I'm checking what's already been optimized—the overlap-logup-precompute-round0 task already saved 64ms by overlapping Round 0 constraint evaluation with GKR input evaluation precompute. Looking at the pipeline between Trace Commit and the constraint phases, those are already overlapped. I need to identify what's still sitting idle and could be parallelized.\n\nThe Constraints phase breaks down into GKR input eval (~300ms), Round 0 (~180ms with some overlap already), GKR fractional sumcheck (~100ms), and MLE rounds (~160ms)—all currently sequential after the initial overlap. The Openings phase is also blocked waiting on Stacked Reduction to finish before WHIR can start. I'm wondering if GKR fractional sumcheck and MLE rounds could run concurrently, but MLE depends on the output from the fractional sumcheck, so that won't work.\n\nNow I'm looking at whether GPU extraction during Round 0 could overlap with GKR input eval. Round 0 processes AIRs across 8 threads—evaluating constraints, extracting polynomials, writing to the batch array—and only after that completes does GKR input eval start. The question is whether we could pipeline this: start GKR input eval on some AIRs while Round 0 is still processing others. But GKR needs all the Round 0 outputs to build its proof structure, so there's a hard dependency there. Actually, let me reconsider—GKR input evaluation might be independent of Round 0 outputs depending on how the fractional sumcheck is structured.\n\nLooking at the actual phase ordering in the prove function, there are three distinct stages: Round 0 evaluates constraints at the quotient point using challenge r, then GKR input eval feeds into the fractional sumcheck using logup challenges, and finally MLE rounds prove the combined zerocheck and logup claim. Each phase depends on the previous one's output, so the sequencing is locked. Let me check the actual implementation to see if there's any flexibility here. I see the GKR precompute overlaps with Round 0 work on a background thread, but the main GKR input evaluation and fractional sumcheck complete first before Round 0 starts. The logup precompute then runs concurrently with Round 0, followed by the MLE rounds, giving me a clearer picture of the actual execution timeline.\n\nLooking at the timing breakdown, the 383ms LogUp GKR phase consists of input evaluation taking about 300ms wall-clock time and fractional sumcheck at roughly 83ms, while Round 0 runs for 181ms across 8 threads with multi-streaming. The precompute overlap saved around 64ms by running concurrently with Round 0, though these numbers already account for that savings.\n\nI'm wondering if there's room to overlap the GKR tree build—which bit-reverses the input and constructs the first two tree layers in about 50ms—with other operations. But it depends on the input eval output finishing first, so that's blocked. Similarly, it can't overlap with the first GKR round since the round needs the tree it produces. Now I'm considering whether Trace Commit and GKR could run concurrently, since Trace Commit just produces the commitment independently.\n\nThe challenge is that GKR input eval needs the challenges derived from that commitment, so it has to wait. But what if I could start GKR input eval for one segment while the other segment's Trace Commit is still running? The segments process sequentially right now, but there might be an opportunity to pipeline them.\n\nLooking at the actual Coordinator::prove function, each segment goes through its full pipeline before the next one starts. The real constraint is that segments share GPU memory—with 15GB peak per segment and only 24GB total, I can't run two concurrently. Plus the transcript order matters for Fiat-Shamir, so each segment's commitment has to be observed before the next segment's proof starts. The segments themselves are independent in terms of proof generation, but the serialization is baked into the architecture.\n\nI've been chasing optimization angles for a while now, and the realistic wins are getting smaller. The NTT is already batched with optimized kernels, so there's not much left to squeeze there. I should focus on something concrete and achievable rather than continuing to hunt for architectural changes that would require major restructuring.\n\nLooking at the profiling data, the CPU NTT reconstruction in the stacked reduction approach is the real bottleneck—it's eating up about 36ms of the 75ms total. Moving that to GPU would be straightforward since GPUs handle NTT operations in 1-5ms, giving us roughly 30ms of savings per proof. The previous attempt failed because it tried to add multi-stream parallelism that broke the VPMM pool state, but this is different—I'm just moving a single CPU operation to GPU without changing how memory gets allocated.\n\nThe problem is this saves equally at both APC 0 and APC 300, so it doesn't help with the scaling issue. I need something that specifically targets the higher APC case. The `precompute_logup_combinations` kernel is running 791 times at APC 300 but much less frequently at APC 0, and it's taking 55ms total. If I can reduce the overhead of that kernel, it would differentially improve the higher APC performance.\n\nLooking at the two variants, the true version takes 35ms while the false version takes 20ms, both across 791 instances. The kernel appears to be launched roughly 28 times per round-segment combination, which tracks with 2 segments × 14 rounds.\n\nEach individual launch is quick (44μs for GLOBAL, 25μs for SCATTER), but the cumulative 55ms across 1582 total launches includes significant overhead—roughly 8-16ms just from kernel launch costs. The kernels are already batched using BlockCtx to process multiple traces per launch, so the 28 launches per round-segment likely reflect separate invocations for GLOBAL versus SCATTER trace types or other logical subdivisions.\n\nBut the numbers don't quite add up. I'd expect around 56 launches per variant (2 types × 14 rounds × 2 segments), yet I'm seeing 791 instances per variant—roughly 7 times higher. That suggests either the kernel is being launched per-trace rather than per-group, or the \"Instances\" metric in Nsight is counting something different than I initially thought. Let me reconsider what's actually being measured here.\n\nThe 791 figure is suspiciously close to the number of traces with interactions, which makes me wonder if each trace is launching the kernel independently. But that still doesn't quite reconcile with the math. Rather than keep chasing this discrepancy, I should probably shift focus and look directly at the per-round computation to identify where the actual waste is happening. And while I'm at it, let me check whether the current stacked_reduction implementation has any GPU work that could be optimized away.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2084,"cache_read_input_tokens":148793,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2084},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":