by round 7, but the MLE sumcheck still runs for all 14 rounds. After traces are fully folded, they only contribute constant terms, yet we're still processing them with per-trace kernels. I can track when traces become fully folded, accumulate their constant contributions, and skip the expensive per-trace kernel calls for subsequent rounds.\n\nIn the sumcheck_polys_batch_eval function, I'd add the accumulated constants directly instead of iterating through dead traces. This cuts down the number of descriptors in late-eval kernels and reduces precompute_logup_combinations calls.\n\nThe performance gains are substantial at APC 300—MLE rounds drop from 161ms to about 130ms, with monomial kernels cutting from 41ms to 15ms by skipping roughly 63% of late trace processing. At APC 0 the improvement is minimal since most traces are large and don't fold early. Overall this nets about 31ms at APC 300 and 3ms at APC 0, improving the ratio from 1.59x to 1.63x.\n\nThat's only a 0.04x bump in ratio, which isn't moving the needle much toward 2x. But stacking this with other optimizations could add up. I should focus on whichever change gives the biggest absolute gain at APC 300, since that's what matters for hitting the target. The batch Round 0 kernel fix with sub-batch dispatch looks most promising at 30-50ms, followed by reducing late-eval trace overhead at 25-35ms and GPU-side stacked reduction at 15-20ms.\n\nI'm leaning toward option 3 since it's moderate complexity with solid impact specifically at APC 300. But wait—option 1 keeps nagging at me. The kernel infrastructure is already in place from the earlier task, and the actual problem is just the hang with >12 AIRs. What if I sidestep the batching issue entirely by grouping AIRs by constraint degree and launching per-AIR kernels with a grid that covers the whole group using a descriptor array?\n\nActually, that's essentially what the batch kernel already does. The real culprit is the shared intermediates buffer causing L2 thrashing. If I keep per-AIR intermediates but launch multiple AIRs per kernel with each block handling a different AIR, I avoid that bottleneck. At APC 300 with 300+ small AIRs and 128 SMs, I'd get 600-1200 blocks total, which should distribute well across the hardware.\n\nBut wait—the multi-streaming approach already interleaves AIRs across 8 streams, so launch overhead isn't the issue. The real bottleneck is GPU compute time itself: with 300 AIRs at 100-500μs each, that's 30-150ms of work spread across 8 streams, giving 4-19ms wall-clock. Yet Round 0 takes 181ms, which means a few large AIRs with height 2^17 are each consuming ~5ms and dominating the runtime.\n\nSo batching small AIRs won't help—I need to target the large ones. I'm going to commit to accumulating constant contributions from fully-folded traces in MLE rounds to skip the per-trace monomial kernel processing entirely.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3150,"cache_read_input_tokens":165370,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3150},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97df75af-c1b3-4850-8566-f6b18608e3d5","uuid":"c97e751b-e9c3-41d2-b92b-7be36ebc096a"}
