{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016DcAipqiFH1mFre6y9fiC4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012Uh3tpiE3MY2SGYeVPLKe2","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0200-cache-monomial-products-for-dead-traces/plan.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10819,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10819,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01J7wTUwCB4kx1fVvip9Z9LN","session_id":"97df75af-c1b3-4850-8566-f6b18608e3d5","uuid":"0bd62a8a-7f02-448c-8986-d94deef650e8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012Uh3tpiE3MY2SGYeVPLKe2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Cache Monomial Products for Dead Traces in MLE Rounds\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate GPU monomial evaluation kernel launches for \"dead\" (height-1) traces in MLE sumcheck rounds by caching per-monomial matrix-value products once and recomputing the weighted sum on CPU each round. At APC 300, this targets ~35-41ms of GPU time across `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` (17ms, 178 instances), `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel` (18ms, 43 instances), and `logup_monomial_kernel` (6ms, 356 instances).\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### MLE round flow (`LogupZerocheckGpu::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`, `mod.rs:1602`)\n10\t\n11\tEach MLE round (14 rounds × 2 segments at APC 300):\n12\t\n13\t1. **Categorize traces** (lines 1639-1875): Iterate all traces. If `round > n_lift` → `late_eval` (height-1). If `round <= n_lift` → `early_eval` (height > 1).\n14\t\n15\t2. **Late logup evaluation** (lines 1880-1900): Build `LogupMonomialBatch` from late traces with interactions → launch `logup_monomial_kernel` on GPU → D2H results → store in `self.logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`.\n16\t\n17\t3. **Late zerocheck evaluation** (lines 1901-1919): Build `ZerocheckMonomialBatch` from late traces with constraints → launch `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` on GPU → D2H results → store in `self.zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`.\n18\t\n19\t4. **Early trace evaluation** (lines 1922-2005): Batched logup + zerocheck via GPU kernels for height > 1 traces.\n20\t\n21\t5. **Combine into sumcheck polynomial** (`compute_batch_s_poly`, lines 2011-2085): For late traces (`round > n_lift`), their contribution is a constant `sp_tail += mu_pows * tilde_eval`. On the first late round (`round == n_lift + 1`), tilde_evals are multiplied by `eq_r_acc` / `eq_sharp_r_acc`.\n22\t\n23\t### Key observation\n24\t\n25\tFor height-1 traces in `late_eval`, the folded matrix is 1×width. The matrix values **do not change** between rounds (fold is a no-op at height 1). Only the `lambda_combinations` (from `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel`) and `lambda_combinations` (zerocheck) change per round.\n26\t\n27\tThe monomial kernel computes: `result = sum_m( lambda_comb[m] * product_j( matrix[j]^exp[m][j] ) )`\n28\t\n29\tThe inner products `product_j(matrix[j]^exp[m][j])` are **constant across rounds** for height-1 traces. Only `lambda_comb[m]` changes.\n30\t\n31\t### Where the GPU work happens\n32\t\n33\t- `LogupMonomialBatch::evaluate()` → launches `logup_monomial_kernel` on GPU, reads matrix values from device, evaluates interaction polynomial. File: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/monomial.rs`\n34\t- `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::evaluate()` → launches `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` / `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel` on GPU, reads matrix values and lambda_combs from device. File: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/monomial.rs`\n35\t\n36\t## Changes\n37\t\n38\t### Step 1: Add cached monomial product storage to `LogupZerocheckGpu`\n39\t\n40\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n41\t\n42\tAdd new fields to the `LogupZerocheckGpu` struct:\n43\t\n44\t```rust\n45\t/// Cached per-monomial products for height-1 traces (zerocheck).\n46\t/// Indexed by trace_idx. None = trace not yet dead.\n47\t/// Each inner Vec has one EF per monomial.\n48\tzc_monomial_cache: Vec<Option<Vec<EF>>>,\n49\t\n50\t/// Cached per-interaction evaluation intermediates for height-1 traces (logup).\n51\t/// Stores (p_eval, q_eval) at the frozen matrix values, before lambda_comb weighting.\n52\t/// None = trace not yet dead.\n53\tlogup_frozen_eval: Vec<Option<(EF, EF)>>,\n54\t```\n55\t\n56\tInitialize both as `vec![None; num_traces]` in the constructor.\n57\t\n58\t### Step 2: Cache monomial products when trace first becomes height-1\n59\t\n60\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, in `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`\n61\t\n62\tFor traces entering `late_eval` for the FIRST time (identified by `round == n_lift + 1` AND `self.zc_monomial_cache[trace_idx].is_none()`):\n63\t\n64\t**Zerocheck path:**\n65\t1. The existing GPU monomial kernel runs this one time (round == n_lift + 1) — this is the initial evaluation.\n66\t2. After D2H of the result, additionally D2H the matrix row values (1 × width, from `self.mat_evals_per_trace[trace_idx]` which is at height=1 after fold).\n67\t3. On CPU, compute the per-monomial products:\n68\t   ```\n69\t   for each monomial m in dag.monomials:\n70\t       product = EF::ONE\n71\t       for each (col_idx, exponent) in m.factors:\n72\t           product *= matrix_values[col_idx].pow(exponent)\n73\t       cache[m] = product\n74\t   ```\n75\t4. Store in `self.zc_monomial_cache[trace_idx] = Some(products)`.\n76\t\n77\t**Logup path:**\n78\t1. Similarly, let the GPU kernel run for the first round.\n79\t2. Cache `self.logup_frozen_eval[trace_idx] = Some((p_result, q_result))` from the GPU output.\n80\t3. Since logup evaluations also depend on lambda_comb which changes, we need to cache the interaction intermediate: the fractional evaluation `(p, q)` from the matrix values alone.\n81\t\n82\tActually, for logup, the evaluation is:\n83\t```\n84\tFor each interaction: num/denom = product over fields of (alpha * value + beta)\n85\tFrac = sum over interactions of weight * num/denom\n86\t```\n87\tThe `(alpha, beta)` are fixed challenges. The `value` fields read from the matrix. Since the matrix is frozen, the entire `Frac(p, q)` is frozen.\n88\t\n89\tBut the `logup_monomial_kernel` computes: `result = sum over interactions of (logup_comb[i] * interaction_eval[i])`, where `logup_comb[i]` changes per round.\n90\t\n91\tSo I need to cache the per-interaction evaluations (not the final sum). Each trace has a small number of interactions (1-10 typically).\n92\t\n93\tRevised logup caching:\n94\t1. D2H the matrix row for the trace\n95\t2. On CPU, evaluate each interaction's numerator and denominator from the matrix values\n96\t3. Cache `Vec<Frac<EF>>` (one per interaction per trace)\n97\t4. Each subsequent round: dot product of cached fracs with logup_comb → final `(p, q)`\n98\t\n99\t### Step 3: Use cached values in subsequent rounds\n100\t\n101\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, in `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`\n102\t\n103\tBefore the existing late_eval processing (lines 1879-1919), partition late_eval traces into:\n104\t- `newly_late`: traces where `self.zc_monomial_cache[trace_idx].is_none()` → process with GPU (first time)\n105\t- `cached_late`: traces where cache exists → process with CPU\n106\t\n107\tFor `cached_late` zerocheck traces:\n108\t1. D2H `self.lambda_combinations[air_idx]` for each unique air_idx among cached traces (small: ~20-50 EF values per AIR, ~30-50 distinct AIRs)\n109\t2. For each trace: `tilde_eval = sum_m(lambda_comb[m] * cached_product[m])` — CPU dot product\n110\t3. Store in `self.zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`\n111\t\n112\tFor `cached_late` logup traces:\n113\t1. D2H `self.logup_combinations[trace_idx]` for each cached trace (small: ~5-10 EF values)\n114\t2. For each trace: compute `(p, q) = sum_i(logup_comb[i] * cached_frac[i])` — CPU dot product\n115\t3. Store in `self.logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`\n116\t\n117\tFor `newly_late` traces: run existing GPU kernels (unchanged).\n118\t\n119\t### Step 4: Skip the `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` for dead traces (optional follow-up)\n120\t\n121\tThe `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` currently runs for ALL traces including dead ones (55ms total). We could add a `skip_mask` to the kernel to avoid processing height-1 traces whose results won't be used by GPU kernels. This is a follow-up optimization, not part of the initial implementation.\n122\t\n123\t## Invariants\n124\t\n125\t1. **Correctness**: `zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]` and `logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]` must have the same values whether computed by GPU or CPU path. The CPU path produces `sum(lambda_comb[m] * cached_product[m])` which is algebraically identical to the GPU monomial kernel output.\n126\t\n127\t2. **First-round handling**: On round == n_lift + 1, the trace uses the GPU kernel (to populate the cache). The `compute_batch_s_poly` function multiplies tilde_evals by `eq_r_acc` / `eq_sharp_r_acc` only on this round. This logic is UNCHANGED — it reads from tilde_evals regardless of how they were computed.\n128\t\n129\t3. **Fold no-op for height-1**: After round == n_lift, the fold kernel produces a height-1 matrix. In subsequent rounds, the fold identifies this trace as non-foldable and skips it. The matrix data persists unchanged.\n130\t\n131\t4. **lambda_combinations freshness**: The D2H of lambda_combinations must happen AFTER the `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` has completed for the current round. Since `fold_mle_evals` (which calls precompute) runs before `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`, this ordering is guaranteed.\n132\t\n133\t5. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0, most traces have large n_lift (close to max_n_lift), so few traces enter late_eval in early rounds. The CPU path handles a small number of traces, adding negligible overhead.\n134\t\n135\t## Measurement Plan\n136\t\n137\t1. Run `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}\n138\t2. Compare MLE Rounds metric: expect ~25-35ms reduction at APC 300 (161ms → 126-136ms)\n139\t3. Compare STARK excl trace: expect ~25-35ms reduction at APC 300 (1129ms → 1094-1104ms)\n140\t4. Verify APC 0 shows no regression (expect <5ms change)\n141\t5. Profile with nsight: verify `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` and `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel` instance counts decrease significantly (from 178+43 to ~30-50, only newly-late traces)\n142\t\n143\t## Rollback Criteria\n144\t\n145\t- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 < 15ms (below half the expected 30ms)\n146\t- APC 0 regression > 10ms on STARK excl trace\n147\t- Correctness failure (proof verification fails at any APC config)\n148\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01J7wTUwCB4kx1fVvip9Z9LN","session_id":"97df75af-c1b3-4850-8566-f6b18608e3d5","uuid":"1a7efd57-34ed-4a17-8e08-fb11fb17b8dc","timestamp":"2026-04-16T00:02:44.637Z"}
