{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016cUErnTNxXzdqHD58hMxb8","type":"tool_result","content":"40\tSecondary reduction: `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` reduces across mono_blocks for same (air, y_int).\n41\t\n42\t**Waste at APC 300**: A typical APC AIR has `num_monomials ≈ 5–15`. With `threads_per_block = 256`:\n43\t- `mono_blocks = 1` (ceil(15/256) = 1)\n44\t- 256 threads per block, 15 active → 5.9% utilization\n45\t- Per trace: `num_y` blocks of 256 threads each, totaling `num_y × 256` threads with `num_y × 15` useful FMAs\n46\t\n47\tnsight profile: `zerocheck_monomial_kernel`: 178 instances, 17ms total, 0.09ms avg.\n48\t\n49\t### LogupMonomialBatch (`batch_mle_monomial.rs:588–793`)\n50\t\n51\tSame pattern. `THREADS_PER_BLOCK_LOGUP = 128`. Block assignment differs slightly (accounts for both numerator and denominator interactions). Same utilization problem.\n52\t\n53\t## Changes\n54\t\n55\t### Change 1: New CUDA kernel — warp-per-trace monomial evaluation\n56\t\n57\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.cu`\n58\t\n59\tAdd `warp_zerocheck_monomial_kernel`:"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01J7wTUwCB4kx1fVvip9Z9LN","session_id":"97df75af-c1b3-4850-8566-f6b18608e3d5","uuid":"e040bed1-9b2b-4315-a2f3-cb3b32b3acfc","timestamp":"2026-04-16T00:04:38.011Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a17027b91f10593f0","tool_use_id":"toolu_01J7wTUwCB4kx1fVvip9Z9LN","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"Review optimization plan","usage":{"total_tokens":76132,"tool_uses":36,"duration_ms":224648},"uuid":"205d0ac7-854f-44c3-a54c-093b1f826558","session_id":"97df75af-c1b3-4850-8566-f6b18608e3d5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J7wTUwCB4kx1fVvip9Z9LN","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 178 instances of `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` across 14 MLE rounds and 2 segments makes sense if each segment has about 600+ traces: many traces enter late_eval at round n_lift+1 (which varies per trace) and get one kernel launch each. With batching, these launch as a few batched kernel calls per round, not 600+ individual launches. The 178 instances is the total count including both late_eval and early_eval (low_early) paths.\n\nNow I have a complete picture. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. DESIGN BLOCKER: The plan's core premise is factually wrong\n\nThe plan claims: \"traces that have been fully folded to height 1 still have GPU monomial evaluation kernels launched every round.\" This is incorrect.\n\nIn `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 1665-1719, the code has two branches for `round > n_lift`:\n- **A.1** (`round == n_lift + 1`): The trace enters `late_eval` for the first and only time. A `TraceCtx` is created and GPU monomial kernels fire. The result is stored in `self.zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]` and `self.logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`.\n- **A.2** (`round > n_lift + 1`): The code only scales the previously stored tilde_evals on the CPU: `*tilde_eval *= r_prev` (line 1712) and similar for logup (lines 1715-1717). No GPU kernel launches. No `TraceCtx` is created. The trace is not added to `late_eval`.\n\nThe GPU monomial kernels fire **exactly once** per trace, at the round it first becomes dead. There is no repeated GPU work to cache away. The entire caching mechanism proposed in Steps 1-3 would only avoid the first (and only) GPU evaluation, which is the evaluation that populates the cache, creating a circular dependency.\n\n### 2. DESIGN BLOCKER: The claimed savings contradict established measurements\n\nThe plan targets \"~35-41ms of GPU time\" from the monomial kernels. The previous task's nsight profiling (`2026-04-14-2130-warp-per-trace-mle-eval/report.md`) measured the total GPU time of these kernels as: `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` 17ms (178 instances), `logup_monomial_kernel` ~6ms, `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel` ~18ms. These 178+43+356 instances include BOTH late_eval AND early_eval paths across ALL 14 MLE rounds and 2 segments. The late_eval fraction (one launch per trace lifetime) is a subset of these totals. Even eliminating 100% of these kernels would save at most ~41ms, but the plan can only target the late_eval subset (one-shot launches), not the recurring early_eval launches.\n\nFurthermore, the log entry for `2026-04-14-2130-warp-per-trace-mle-eval` concluded: \"The zerocheck_monomial_kernel itself accounts for only ~17ms of the 158ms MLE Rounds total. Even a hypothetical 100% elimination of kernel time would save at most 17ms.\" The plan claims 30-35ms savings, which is internally inconsistent with the actual kernel time data.\n\n### 3. `lambda_combinations` and `logup_combinations` do NOT change per round\n\nThe plan's Section \"Key observation\" states: \"Only the `lambda_combinations` change per round.\" This is false. Looking at the code:\n- `lambda_combinations` is computed once in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` (line 969) via `compute_lambda_combinations` and is never recomputed during MLE rounds.\n- `logup_combinations` is computed once at Round 0 setup (lines 1326/1391) via `compute_logup_combinations` and is never recomputed.\n\nBoth are fixed for the entire MLE phase. So the plan's premise -- that a CPU dot product with fresh lambda_combs each round would replace GPU work -- is based on a misunderstanding. There are no fresh weights to apply.\n\n### 4. Step 4's reference to `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` running per round is fabricated\n\nStep 4 says: \"The `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` currently runs for ALL traces including dead ones (55ms total).\" No such per-round kernel exists. The precomputation runs once at Round 0 and the results are stored for the entire MLE phase. The 55ms figure and the idea of adding a `skip_mask` have no basis in the current code.\n\n### 5. File path error\n\nThe plan references `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/monomial.rs` in two locations (lines 33-34). This file does not exist. The actual file is `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs`.\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n\nThe MLE Rounds phase is firmly kernel-execution-bound at ~162ms (per the most recent log entry `2026-04-16-0030-hoist-mle-trace-ctx-construction`). The monomial evaluation kernels total ~41ms, of which only a fraction comes from late_eval. Realistic improvements at this point would need to:\n\n1. **Reduce total kernel execution time** by fusing multiple MLE round operations (interpolate + fold + evaluate) into fewer kernel launches, or by implementing persistent kernels that avoid launch overhead entirely.\n\n2. **Target the early_eval path** rather than late_eval. Early_eval traces run GPU kernels every round they are alive (height > 1), so the cumulative work is proportional to round count multiplied by surviving trace count. Late_eval traces run once and cost nearly nothing afterward.\n\n3. **Address the non-scaling components** identified in the project context. MLE Rounds is only 162ms at APC 300 -- improving it by even 20% would save ~32ms. The bigger scaling gaps are likely in LogUp GKR (~520ms) and Round 0 (~180ms).\n\n## Assessment\n\n**There is a design-level correctness blocker.** The plan's fundamental premise -- that GPU monomial kernels fire repeatedly for dead traces -- is wrong. The code already handles dead traces efficiently: one GPU evaluation at the round they become dead, then pure CPU scaling (`*= r_prev`) in all subsequent rounds. The caching mechanism is solving a problem that does not exist.\n\n**The plan is not prototype-ready.** Implementing it as written would produce a cache that is populated in the same round it would be used, making the cache redundant. The \"subsequent rounds\" that would benefit from caching (A.2) already skip GPU entirely.\n\n**Strongest aspect of the plan:** The analysis of the monomial kernel's inner structure (per-monomial products being constant for frozen matrices) is algebraically correct. If the GPU kernels did fire repeatedly, the proposed factorization would be valid. The error is in the outer control flow, not the algebraic reasoning."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a17027b91f10593f0 (use SendMessage with to: 'a17027b91f10593f0' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 77602\ntool_uses: 36\nduration_ms: 224649</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97df75af-c1b3-4850-8566-f6b18608e3d5","uuid":"65a2fe9b-6eea-4f2c-bcf2-be4a7dda7ba9","timestamp":"2026-04-16T00:05:19.426Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are a plan reviewer for a GPU prover optimization project. Review the following plan following the review guideline.\n\nRead these files:\n1. `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt/review_guideline.md` — the review standard to follow\n2. `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt/context.md` — project context\n3. `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md` — the task being planned\n4. `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0200-cache-monomial-products-for-dead-traces/plan.md` — the plan to review\n\nAdditionally, you should verify the plan against the actual codebase:\n- Check `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` to verify the code paths described in the plan match reality\n- Check `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/monomial.rs` to understand what the monomial kernels actually compute\n- Verify the `compute_batch_s_poly` function handles late traces as described\n- Check how `lambda_combinations` and `logup_combinations` are stored and updated\n\nThe key questions to answer:\n1. Is the claim correct that matrix values are constant for height-1 traces across MLE rounds?\n2. Is the caching mechanism correctly designed (what exactly to cache, when to cache it)?\n3. Are the estimated savings realistic given the nsight profiling data?\n4. Are there hidden interface mismatches or correctness issues?\n\nReturn your response in the format: Findings, Improvements, Assessment.\n","agentId":"a17027b91f10593f0","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 178 instances of `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` across 14 MLE rounds and 2 segments makes sense if each segment has about 600+ traces: many traces enter late_eval at round n_lift+1 (which varies per trace) and get one kernel launch each. With batching, these launch as a few batched kernel calls per round, not 600+ individual launches. The 178 instances is the total count including both late_eval and early_eval (low_early) paths.\n\nNow I have a complete picture. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. DESIGN BLOCKER: The plan's core premise is factually wrong\n\nThe plan claims: \"traces that have been fully folded to height 1 still have GPU monomial evaluation kernels launched every round.\" This is incorrect.\n\nIn `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 1665-1719, the code has two branches for `round > n_lift`:\n- **A.1** (`round == n_lift + 1`): The trace enters `late_eval` for the first and only time. A `TraceCtx` is created and GPU monomial kernels fire. The result is stored in `self.zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]` and `self.logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`.\n- **A.2** (`round > n_lift + 1`): The code only scales the previously stored tilde_evals on the CPU: `*tilde_eval *= r_prev` (line 1712) and similar for logup (lines 1715-1717). No GPU kernel launches. No `TraceCtx` is created. The trace is not added to `late_eval`.\n\nThe GPU monomial kernels fire **exactly once** per trace, at the round it first becomes dead. There is no repeated GPU work to cache away. The entire caching mechanism proposed