 round, we eliminate the GPU kernel launches and D2H syncs, saving ~30-35ms at APC 300 with minimal impact at APC 0 where fewer traces are height-1.","+The Stacked Reduction's `fold_ple_evals` function launches one `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` GPU kernel per unstacked trace in a sequential loop (lines 714-742 of `stacked_reduction.rs`). At APC 300, this creates ~600-800 individual kernel launches per proof across 2 segments. Each launch averages 12μs but serializes on the GPU stream, preventing concurrent SM utilization across traces. Replacing the per-trace loop with a single descriptor-array batched kernel launch enables concurrent processing of all traces on different SMs, converting the current sum-of-kernel-times (~9.5ms) into a max-of-kernel-times (~2ms) while also eliminating ~790 kernel launch overhead cycles (~4ms). Expected savings: ~10ms at APC 300."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-16-0200-cache-monomial-products-for-dead-traces\n\nIn MLE sumcheck rounds, traces that have been fully folded to height 1 (\"late_eval\" / dead traces) still have GPU monomial evaluation kernels launched every round (zerocheck_monomial_kernel: 17ms, zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel: 18ms, logup_monomial_kernel: 6ms = ~41ms total at APC 300). For these traces, the matrix values are constant across rounds — only the lambda_comb weights change. By caching the per-monomial products once when a trace first becomes height-1 and computing the weighted sum on CPU each subsequent round, we eliminate the GPU kernel launches and D2H syncs, saving ~30-35ms at APC 300 with minimal impact at APC 0 where fewer traces are height-1.\n"}}
