{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Cv7sbPDDjNLQyiCxS2QoVr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015f7UFPLyySQkhq94UoXkkg","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0200-cache-monomial-products-for-dead-traces/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Batch fold_ple Kernel Launches via Descriptor Array\n\n## Goal\n\nReplace the per-trace sequential loop of `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` GPU kernel launches in `fold_ple_evals` (stacked_reduction.rs:714-742) with a single batched descriptor-array kernel launch. At APC 300 with ~600-800 traces across 2 segments, this eliminates ~790 individual kernel launches and enables concurrent SM utilization across traces.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Rust side: `StackedReductionGpu::fold_ple_evals` (`stacked_reduction.rs:688-788`)\n\nAfter Round 0, this function folds PLE evaluations for each unstacked trace:\n\n```rust\nfor stacked in &self.stacked_per_commit {           // ~2-3 commit groups\n    let folded_evals = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(num_x * stacked_width);\n    folded_evals.fill_zero()?;\n    let mut dst_offset = 0;\n    for trace in &stacked.traces {                   // ~300 traces at APC 300\n        stacked_reduction_fold_ple(                  // <-- One kernel launch per trace\n            trace.buffer().as_ptr(), dst, ...,\n            trace.height(), trace.width(), l_skip,\n        )?;\n        dst_offset += new_height * trace.width();\n    }\n    self.q_evals.push(folded_evals);\n}\n```\n\nEach `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` call launches `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` with grid `(ceil(new_height / chunks_per_block), trace_width)` and block `(max(256, skip_domain))`.\n\n### CUDA kernel: `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` (`stacked_reduction.cu:175-229`)\n\nGrid: 2D — `blockIdx.x` selects row chunk, `blockIdx.y` selects column.\nPer-thread: barycentric interpolation of `skip_domain` source values into one output FpExt value.\n\nParameters per trace: `src` (source trace pointer), `dst` (output pointer within folded_evals buffer), `trace_height`, `new_height`, `skip_domain`.\n\n### Why it's slow\n\nnsight profiling shows `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel<false>` (same kernel, different instantiation): **797 instances, 9.5ms total, avg 12μs per instance**.\n\nTwo costs:\n1. **Kernel launch overhead**: ~5μs × 797 = ~4ms. Each launch goes through the CUDA driver.\n2. **Sequential SM utilization**: Each per-trace kernel runs alone on the GPU. Small traces (low width × low height) use few blocks, leaving most of the 128 SMs idle. The next trace's kernel only starts after the previous completes.\n\nWith a batched kernel, all traces' blocks launch simultaneously. The GPU scheduler distributes them across SMs concurrently, converting sum-of-times into max-of-times.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Step 1: Define descriptor struct\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\nAdd a `FoldPleDesc` descriptor:\n\n```c\nstruct FoldPleDesc {\n    const Fp *src;          // source trace pointer\n    FpExt *dst;             // destination within folded_evals buffer\n    uint32_t trace_height;\n    uint32_t new_height;\n    uint32_t trace_width;\n};\n```\n\n### Step 2: Add BlockCtx mapping struct\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\nAdd a `FoldPleBlockCtx` that maps each CUDA block to a (descriptor_index, local_block_x, column_idx):\n\n```c\nstruct FoldPleBlockCtx {\n    uint32_t desc_idx;      // index into FoldPleDesc array\n    uint32_t local_block_x; // row chunk index within this trace\n    uint32_t col_idx;       // column index within this trace\n};\n```\n\n### Step 3: Implement batched kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\nAdd `batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel`:\n\n```c\n__global__ void batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel(\n    const FoldPleBlockCtx *block_ctx,\n    const FoldPleDesc *descs,\n    const Fp *omega_skip_pows,\n    const FpExt *inv_lagrange_denoms,\n    uint32_t skip_domain\n) {\n    auto ctx = block_ctx[blockIdx.x];\n    auto desc = descs[ctx.desc_idx];\n    \n    uint32_t col_idx = ctx.col_idx;\n    uint32_t chunks_per_block = blockDim.x / skip_domain;\n    uint32_t chunk_in_block = threadIdx.x / skip_domain;\n    uint32_t tid_in_chunk = threadIdx.x % skip_domain;\n    uint32_t row_idx = ctx.local_block_x * chunks_per_block + chunk_in_block;\n    \n    // Same body as stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel, but reading from desc\n    bool const active_chunk = (row_idx < desc.new_height);\n    // ... (barycentric interpolation using desc.src, desc.trace_height, desc.trace_width)\n    // Output to desc.dst at appropriate offset\n}\n```\n\nThe block body is identical to the existing `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` but reads `src`, `dst`, `trace_height`, `new_height` from the descriptor instead of kernel parameters.\n\n### Step 4: Add C launcher function\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\n```c\nextern \"C\" int _batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple(\n    const FoldPleBlockCtx *block_ctx,\n    const FoldPleDesc *descs,\n    uint32_t total_blocks,\n    const Fp *omega_skip_pows,\n    const FpExt *inv_lagrange_denoms,\n    uint32_t l_skip\n) {\n    uint32_t skip_domain = 1u << l_skip;\n    uint32_t block_size = std::max(256u, skip_domain);\n    uint32_t total_warps = block_size / WARP_SIZE;\n    size_t smem_bytes = (skip_domain > WARP_SIZE) ? total_warps * sizeof(FpExt) : 0;\n    \n    batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel<<<total_blocks, block_size, smem_bytes>>>(\n        block_ctx, descs, omega_skip_pows, inv_lagrange_denoms, skip_domain\n    );\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n### Step 5: Add Rust FFI bindings\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nAdd `#[repr(C)]` structs for `FoldPleDesc` and `FoldPleBlockCtx`, plus the extern \"C\" declaration and safe wrapper:\n\n```rust\n#[repr(C)]\npub struct FoldPleDesc {\n    pub src: *const F,\n    pub dst: *mut EF,\n    pub trace_height: u32,\n    pub new_height: u32,\n    pub trace_width: u32,\n}\n\n#[repr(C)]\npub struct FoldPleBlockCtx {\n    pub desc_idx: u32,\n    pub local_block_x: u32,\n    pub col_idx: u32,\n}\n```\n\n### Step 6: Replace per-trace loop with batched dispatch\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, in `fold_ple_evals`\n\nReplace lines 714-742 (the per-trace loop) with:\n\n1. **Build descriptors** (CPU): Iterate traces, compute `new_height`, `dst_offset`, create `FoldPleDesc` per trace.\n\n2. **Build BlockCtx array** (CPU): For each descriptor, compute the grid that the per-trace kernel would have used: `grid_x = ceil(new_height / chunks_per_block)`, `grid_y = trace_width`. Create one `FoldPleBlockCtx` per block.\n\n3. **Upload** descriptors and BlockCtx arrays to device (`to_device()`).\n\n4. **Single kernel launch** with `total_blocks = sum of all per-trace block counts`.\n\n5. **Fallback**: If `traces.len() < 10` (e.g., APC 0 with few traces), use the existing per-trace loop to avoid descriptor overhead.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Output equivalence**: Each trace's folded output in `folded_evals` must be identical to the per-trace kernel output. The batched kernel uses the exact same computation per block.\n\n2. **Destination non-overlap**: Each trace writes to a distinct region of `folded_evals` (offset by `dst_offset`). The batched kernel preserves this since each descriptor has its own `dst` pointer.\n\n3. **omega_skip_pows and inv_lagrange_denoms**: Shared across all traces (same for the entire fold_ple_evals call). Passed as kernel parameters, not per-descriptor.\n\n4. **Block size consistency**: The batched kernel uses the same block size as the per-trace kernel (`max(256, skip_domain)`). Shared memory allocation is the same.\n\n5. **APC 0 safety**: The fallback path ensures APC 0 (few traces, below threshold) is unaffected.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Run benchmarks for APC {0, 100, 300} using `run_pairing.sh`.\n2. Compare Stacked Reduction metric: expect ~8-12ms reduction at APC 300 (75ms → 63-67ms).\n3. Compare STARK excl trace: expect ~8-12ms reduction at APC 300 (1129ms → 1117-1121ms).\n4. Verify APC 0 shows no regression (fallback path active).\n5. nsight: verify `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel` instance count drops from ~797 to ~2-4 (one batched launch per commit group per segment).\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- Stacked Reduction improvement at APC 300 < 5ms\n- APC 0 regression > 5ms on Stacked Reduction\n- Correctness failure at any APC configuration\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2157,"cache_read_input_tokens":220283,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2157},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97df75af-c1b3-4850-8566-f6b18608e3d5","uuid":"8e123c6b-7a33-417f-9d05-cc3313e2d8f0"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015f7UFPLyySQkhq94UoXkkg","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0200-cache-monomial-products-for-dead-traces/plan.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97df75af-c1b3-4850-8566-f6b18608e3d5","uuid":"50f959e8-bf66-4fe3-b97d-7f49363485a4","timestamp":"2026-04-16T00:12:42.079Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-0200-cache-monomial-products-for-dead-traces/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Batch fold_ple Kernel Launches via Descriptor Array\n\n## Goal\n\nReplace the per-trace sequential loop of `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` GPU kernel launches in `fold_ple_evals` (stacked_reduction.rs:714-742) with a single batched descriptor-array kernel launch. At APC 300 with ~600-800 traces across 2 segments, this eliminates ~790 individual kernel launches and enables concurrent SM utilization across traces.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Rust side: `StackedReductionGpu::fold_ple_evals` (`stacked_reduction.rs:688-788`)\n\nAfter Round 0, this function folds PLE evaluations for each unstacked trace:\n\n```rust\nfor stacked in &self.stacked_per_commit {           // ~2-3 commit groups\n    let folded_evals = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(num_x * stacked_width);\n    folded_evals.fill_zero()?;\n    let mut dst_offset = 0;\n    for trace in &stacked.traces {                   // ~300 traces at APC 300\n        stacked_reduction_fold_ple(                  // <-- One kernel launch per trace\n            trace.buffer().as_ptr(), dst, ...,\n            trace.height(), trace.width(), l_skip,\n        )?;\n        dst_offset += new_height * trace.width();\n    }\n    self.q_evals.push(folded_evals);\n}\n```\n\nEach `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` call launches `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` with grid `(ceil(new_height / chunks_per_block), trace_width)` and block `(max(256, skip_domain))`.\n\n### CUDA kernel: `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` (`stacked_reduction.cu:175-229`)\n\nGrid: 2D — `blockIdx.x` selects row chunk, `blockIdx.y` selects column.\nPer-thread: barycentric interpolation of `skip_domain` source values into one output FpExt value.\n\nParameters per trace: `src` (source trace pointer), `dst` (output pointer within folded_evals buffer), `trace_height`, `new_height`, `skip_domain`.\n\n### Why it's slow\n\nnsight profiling shows `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel<false>` (same kernel, different instantiation): **797 instances, 9.5ms total, avg 12μs per instance**.\n\nTwo costs:\n1. **Kernel launch overhead**: ~5μs × 797 = ~4ms. Each launch goes through the CUDA driver.\n2. **Sequential SM utilization**: Each per-trace kernel runs alone on the GPU. Small traces (low width × low height) use few blocks, leaving most of the 128 SMs idle. The next trace's kernel only starts after the previous completes.\n\nWith a batched kernel, all traces' blocks launch simultaneously. The GPU scheduler distributes them across SMs concurrently, converting sum-of-times into max-of-times.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Step 1: Define descriptor struct\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\nAdd a `FoldPleDesc` descriptor:\n\n```c\nstruct FoldPleDesc {\n    const Fp *src;          // source trace pointer\n    FpExt *dst;             // destination within folded_evals buffer\n    uint32_t trace_height;\n    uint32_t new_height;\n    uint32_t trace_width;\n};\n```\n\n### Step 2: Add BlockCtx mapping struct\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\nAdd a `FoldPleBlockCtx` that maps each CUDA block to a (descriptor_index, local_block_x, column_idx):\n\n```c\nstruct FoldPleBlockCtx {\n    uint32_t desc_idx;      // index into FoldPleDesc array\n    uint32_t local_block_x; // row chunk index within this trace\n    uint32_t col_idx;       // column index within this trace\n};\n```\n\n### Step 3: Implement batched kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\nAdd `batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel`:\n\n```c\n__global__ void batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel(\n    const FoldPleBlockCtx *block_ctx,\n    const FoldPleDesc *descs,\n    const Fp *omega_skip_pows,\n    const FpExt *inv_lagrange_denoms,\n    uint32_t skip_domain\n) {\n    auto ctx = block_ctx[blockIdx.x];\n    auto desc = descs[ctx.desc_idx];\n    \n    uint32_t col_idx = ctx.col_idx;\n    uint32_t chunks_per_block = blockDim.x / skip_domain;\n    uint32_t chunk_in_block = threadIdx.x / skip_domain;\n    uint32_t tid_in_chunk = threadIdx.x % skip_domain;\n    uint32_t row_idx = ctx.local_block_x * chunks_per_block + chunk_in_block;\n    \n    // Same body as stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel, but reading from desc\n    bool const active_chunk = (row_idx < desc.new_height);\n    // ... (barycentric interpolation using desc.src, desc.trace_height, desc.trace_width)\n    // Output to desc.dst at appropriate offset\n}\n```\n\nThe block body is identical to the existing `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` but reads `src`, `dst`, `trace_height`, `new_height` from the descriptor instead of kernel parameters.\n\n### Step 4: Add C launcher function\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n\n```c\nextern \"C\" int _batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple(\n    const FoldPleBlockCtx *block_ctx,\n    const FoldPleDesc *descs,\n    uint32_t total_blocks,\n    const Fp *omega_skip_pows,\n    const FpExt *inv_lagrange_denoms,\n    uint32_t l_skip\n) {\n    uint32_t skip_domain = 1u << l_skip;\n    uint32_t block_size = std::max(256u, skip_domain);\n    uint32_t total_warps = block_size / WARP_SIZE;\n    size_t smem_bytes = (skip_domain > WARP_SIZE) ? total_warps * sizeof(FpExt) : 0;\n    \n    batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel<<<total_blocks, block_size, smem_bytes>>>(\n        block_ctx, descs, omega_skip_pows, inv_lagrange_denoms, skip_domain\n    );\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n### Step 5: Add Rust FFI bindings\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n\nAdd `#[repr(C)]` structs for `FoldPleDesc` and `FoldPleBlockCtx`, plus the extern \"C\" declaration and safe wrapper:\n\n```rust\n#[repr(C)]\npub struct FoldPleDesc {\n    pub src: *const F,\n    pub dst: *mut EF,\n    pub trace_height: u32,\n    pub new_height: u32,\n    pub trace_width: u32,\n}\n\n#[repr(C)]\npub struct FoldPleBlockCtx {\n    pub desc_idx: u32,\n    pub local_block_x: u32,\n    pub col_idx: u32,\n}\n```\n\n### Step 6: Replace per-trace loop with batched dispatch\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, in `fold_ple_evals`\n\nReplace lines 714-742 (the per-trace loop) with:\n\n1. **Build descriptors** (CPU): Iterate traces, compute `new_height`, `dst_offset`, create `FoldPleDesc` per trace.\n\n2. **Build BlockCtx array** (CPU): For each descriptor, compute the grid that the per-trace kernel would have used: `grid_x = ceil(new_height / chunks_per_block)`, `grid_y = trace_width`. Create one `FoldPleBlockCtx` per block.\n\n3. **Upload** descriptors and BlockCtx arrays to device (`to_device()`).\n\n4. **Single kernel launch** with `total_blocks = sum of all per-trace block counts`.\n\n5. **Fallback**: If `traces.len() < 10` (e.g., APC 0 with few traces), use the existing per-trace loop to avoid descriptor overhead.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Output equivalence**: Each trace's folded output in `folded_evals` must be identical to the per-trace kernel output. The batched kernel uses the exact same computation per block.\n\n2. **Destination non-overlap**: Each trace writes to a distinct region of `folded_evals` (offset by `dst_offset`). The batched kernel preserves this since each descriptor has its own `dst` pointer.\n\n3. **omega_skip_pows and inv_lagrange_denoms**: Shared across all traces (same for the entire fold_ple_evals call). Passed as kernel parameters, not per-descriptor.\n\n4. **Block size consistency**: The batched kernel uses the same block size as the per-trace kernel (`max(256, skip_domain)`). Shared memory allocation is the same.\n\n5. **APC 0 safety**: The fallback path ensures APC 0 (few traces, below threshold) is unaffected.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Run benchmarks for APC {0, 100, 300} using `run_pairing.sh`.\n2. Compare Stacked Reduction metric: expect ~8-12ms reduction at APC 300 (75ms → 63-67ms).\n3. Compare STARK excl trace: expect ~8-12ms reduction at APC 300 (1129ms → 1117-1121ms).\n4. Verify APC 0 shows no regression (fallback path active).\n5. nsight: verify `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel` instance count drops from ~797 to ~2-4 (one batched launch per commit group per segment).\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- Stacked Reduction improvement at APC 300 < 5ms\n- APC 0 regression > 5ms on Stacked Reduction\n- Correctness failure at any APC configuration\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":147,"newStart":1,"newLines":201,"lines":["-# Plan: Cache Monomial Products for Dead Traces in MLE Rounds","+# Plan: Batch fold_ple Kernel Launches via Descriptor Array"," "," ## Goal"," ","-Eliminate GPU monomial evaluation kernel launches for \"dead\" (height-1) traces in MLE sumcheck rounds by caching per-monomial matrix-value products once and recomputing the weighted sum on CPU each round. At APC 300, this targets ~35-41ms of GPU time across `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` (17ms, 178 instances), `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel` (18ms, 43 instances), and `logup_monomial_kernel` (6ms, 356 instances).","+Replace the per-trace sequential loop of `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` GPU kernel launches in `fold_ple_evals` (stacked_reduction.rs:714-742) with a single batched descriptor-array kernel launch. At APC 300 with ~600-800 traces across 2 segments, this eliminates ~790 individual kernel launches and enables concurrent SM utilization across traces."," "," ## Current Code Path"," ","-### MLE round flow (`LogupZerocheckGpu::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`, `mod.rs:1602`)","+### Rust side: `StackedReductionGpu::fold_ple_evals` (`stacked_reduction.rs:688-788`)"," ","-Each MLE round (14 rounds × 2 segments at APC 300):","+After Round 0, this function folds PLE evaluations for each unstacked trace:"," ","-1. **Categorize traces** (lines 1639-1875): Iterate all traces. If `round > n_lift` → `late_eval` (height-1). If `round <= n_lift` → `early_eval` (height > 1).","+```rust","+for stacked in &self.stacked_per_commit {           // ~2-3 commit groups","+    let folded_evals = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(num_x * stacked_width);","+    folded_evals.fill_zero()?;","+    let mut dst_offset = 0;","+    for trace in &stacked.traces {                   // ~300 traces at APC 300","+        stacked_reduction_fold_ple(                  // <-- One kernel launch per trace","+            trace.buffer().as_ptr(), dst, ...,","+            trace.height(), trace.width(), l_skip,","+        )?;","+        dst_offset += new_height * trace.width();","+    }","+    self.q_evals.push(folded_evals);","+}","+```"," ","-2. **Late logup evaluation** (lines 1880-1900): Build `LogupMonomialBatch` from late traces with interactions → launch `logup_monomial_kernel` on GPU → D2H results → store in `self.logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`.","+Each `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` call launches `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` with grid `(ceil(new_height / chunks_per_block), trace_width)` and block `(max(256, skip_domain))`."," ","-3. **Late zerocheck evaluation** (lines 1901-1919): Build `ZerocheckMonomialBatch` from late traces with constraints → launch `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` on GPU → D2H results → store in `self.zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`.","+### CUDA kernel: `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` (`stacked_reduction.cu:175-229`)"," ","-4. **Early trace evaluation** (lines 1922-2005): Batched logup + zerocheck via GPU kernels for height > 1 traces.","+Grid: 2D — `blockIdx.x` selects row chunk, `blockIdx.y` selects column.","+Per-thread: barycentric interpolation of `skip_domain` source values into one output FpExt value."," ","-5. **Combine into sumcheck polynomial** (`compute_batch_s_poly`, lines 2011-2085): For late traces (`round > n_lift`), their contribution is a constant `sp_tail += mu_pows * tilde_eval`. On the first late round (`round == n_lift + 1`), tilde_evals are multiplied by `eq_r_acc` / `eq_sharp_r_acc`.","+Parameters per trace: `src` (source trace pointer), `dst` (output pointer within folded_evals buffer), `trace_height`, `new_height`, `skip_domain`."," ","-### Key observation","+### Why it's slow"," ","-For height-1 traces in `late_eval`, the folded matrix is 1×width. The matrix values **do not change** between rounds (fold is a no-op at height 1). Only the `lambda_combinations` (from `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel`) and `lambda_combinations` (zerocheck) change per round.","+nsight profiling shows `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel<false>` (same kernel, different instantiation): **797 instances, 9.5ms total, avg 12μs per instance**."," ","-The monomial kernel computes: `result = sum_m( lambda_comb[m] * product_j( matrix[j]^exp[m][j] ) )`","+Two costs:","+1. **Kernel launch overhead**: ~5μs × 797 = ~4ms. Each launch goes through the CUDA driver.","+2. **Sequential SM utilization**: Each per-trace kernel runs alone on the GPU. Small traces (low width × low height) use few blocks, leaving most of the 128 SMs idle. The next trace's kernel only starts after the previous completes."," ","-The inner products `product_j(matrix[j]^exp[m][j])` are **constant across rounds** for height-1 traces. Only `lambda_comb[m]` changes.","+With a batched kernel, all traces' blocks launch simultaneously. The GPU scheduler distributes them across SMs concurrently, converting sum-of-times into max-of-times."," ","-### Where the GPU work happens","+## Changes"," ","-- `LogupMonomialBatch::evaluate()` → launches `logup_monomial_kernel` on GPU, reads matrix values from device, evaluates interaction polynomial. File: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/monomial.rs`","-- `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::evaluate()` → launches `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` / `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel` on GPU, reads matrix values and lambda_combs from device. File: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/monomial.rs`","+### Step 1: Define descriptor struct"," ","-## Changes","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`"," ","-### Step 1: Add cached monomial product storage to `LogupZerocheckGpu`","+Add a `FoldPleDesc` descriptor:"," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`","+```c","+struct FoldPleDesc {","+    const Fp *src;          // source trace pointer","+    FpExt *dst;             // destination within folded_evals buffer","+    uint32_t trace_height;","+    uint32_t new_height;","+    uint32_t trace_width;","+};","+```"," ","-Add new fields to the `LogupZerocheckGpu` struct:","+### Step 2: Add BlockCtx mapping struct"," ","-```rust","-/// Cached per-monomial products for height-1 traces (zerocheck).","-/// Indexed by trace_idx. None = trace not yet dead.","-/// Each inner Vec has one EF per monomial.","-zc_monomial_cache: Vec<Option<Vec<EF>>>,","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`"," ","-/// Cached per-interaction evaluation intermediates for height-1 traces (logup).","-/// Stores (p_eval, q_eval) at the frozen matrix values, before lambda_comb weighting.","-/// None = trace not yet dead.","-logup_frozen_eval: Vec<Option<(EF, EF)>>,","+Add a `FoldPleBlockCtx` that maps each CUDA block to a (descriptor_index, local_block_x, column_idx):","+","+```c","+struct FoldPleBlockCtx {","+    uint32_t desc_idx;      // index into FoldPleDesc array","+    uint32_t local_block_x; // row chunk index within this trace","+    uint32_t col_idx;       // column index within this trace","+};"," ```"," ","-Initialize both as `vec![None; num_traces]` in the constructor.","+### Step 3: Implement batched kernel"," ","-### Step 2: Cache monomial products when trace first becomes height-1","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`"," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, in `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`","+Add `batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel`:"," ","-For traces entering `late_eval` for the FIRST time (identified by `round == n_lift + 1` AND `self.zc_monomial_cache[trace_idx].is_none()`):","+```c","+__global__ void batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel(","+    const FoldPleBlockCtx *block_ctx,","+    const FoldPleDesc *descs,","+    const Fp *omega_skip_pows,","+    const FpExt *inv_lagrange_denoms,","+    uint32_t skip_domain","+) {","+    auto ctx = block_ctx[blockIdx.x];","+    auto desc = descs[ctx.desc_idx];","+    ","+    uint32_t col_idx = ctx.col_idx;","+    uint32_t chunks_per_block = blockDim.x / skip_domain;","+    uint32_t chunk_in_block = threadIdx.x / skip_domain;","+    uint32_t tid_in_chunk = threadIdx.x % skip_domain;","+    uint32_t row_idx = ctx.local_block_x * chunks_per_block + chunk_in_block;","+    ","+    // Same body as stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel, but reading from desc","+    bool const active_chunk = (row_idx < desc.new_height);","+    // ... (barycentric interpolation using desc.src, desc.trace_height, desc.trace_width)","+    // Output to desc.dst at appropriate offset","+}","+```"," ","-**Zerocheck path:**","-1. The existing GPU monomial kernel runs this one time (round == n_lift + 1) — this is the initial evaluation.","-2. After D2H of the result, additionally D2H the matrix row values (1 × width, from `self.mat_evals_per_trace[trace_idx]` which is at height=1 after fold).","-3. On CPU, compute the per-monomial products:","-   ```","-   for each monomial m in dag.monomials:","-       product = EF::ONE","-       for each (col_idx, exponent) in m.factors:","-           product *= matrix_values[col_idx].pow(exponent)","-       cache[m] = product","-   ```","-4. Store in `self.zc_monomial_cache[trace_idx] = Some(products)`.","+The block body is identical to the existing `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` but reads `src`, `dst`, `trace_height`, `new_height` from the descriptor instead of kernel parameters."," ","-**Logup path:**","-1. Similarly, let the GPU kernel run for the first round.","-2. Cache `self.logup_frozen_eval[trace_idx] = Some((p_result, q_result))` from the GPU output.","-3. Since logup evaluations also depend on lambda_comb which changes, we need to cache the interaction intermediate: the fractional evaluation `(p, q)` from the matrix values alone.","+### Step 4: Add C launcher function"," ","-Actually, for logup, the evaluation is:","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`","+","+```c","+extern \"C\" int _batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple(","+    const FoldPleBlockCtx *block_ctx,","+    const FoldPleDesc *descs,","+    uint32_t total_blocks,","+    const Fp *omega_skip_pows,","+    const FpExt *inv_lagrange_denoms,","+    uint32_t l_skip","+) {","+    uint32_t skip_domain = 1u << l_skip;","+    uint32_t block_size = std::max(256u, skip_domain);","+    uint32_t total_warps = block_size / WARP_SIZE;","+    size_t smem_bytes = (skip_domain > WARP_SIZE) ? total_warps * sizeof(FpExt) : 0;","+    ","+    batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel<<<total_blocks, block_size, smem_bytes>>>(","+        block_ctx, descs, omega_skip_pows, inv_lagrange_denoms, skip_domain","+    );","+    return CHECK_KERNEL();","+}"," ```","-For each interaction: num/denom = product over fields of (alpha * value + beta)","-Frac = sum over interactions of weight * num/denom","-```","-The `(alpha, beta)` are fixed challenges. The `value` fields read from the matrix. Since the matrix is frozen, the entire `Frac(p, q)` is frozen."," ","-But the `logup_monomial_kernel` computes: `result = sum over interactions of (logup_comb[i] * interaction_eval[i])`, where `logup_comb[i]` changes per round.","+### Step 5: Add Rust FFI bindings"," ","-So I need to cache the per-interaction evaluations (not the final sum). Each trace has a small number of interactions (1-10 typically).","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`"," ","-Revised logup caching:","-1. D2H the matrix row for the trace","-2. On CPU, evaluate each interaction's numerator and denominator from the matrix values","-3. Cache `Vec<Frac<EF>>` (one per interaction per trace)","-4. Each subsequent round: dot product of cached fracs with logup_comb → final `(p, q)`","+Add `#[repr(C)]` structs for `FoldPleDesc` and `FoldPleBlockCtx`, plus the extern \"C\" declaration and safe wrapper:"," ","-### Step 3: Use cached values in subsequent rounds","+```rust","+#[repr(C)]","+pub struct FoldPleDesc {","+    pub src: *const F,","+    pub dst: *mut EF,","+    pub trace_height: u32,","+    pub new_height: u32,","+    pub trace_width: u32,","+}"," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, in `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`","+#[repr(C)]","+pub struct FoldPleBlockCtx {","+    pub desc_idx: u32,","+    pub local_block_x: u32,","+    pub col_idx: u32,","+}","+```"," ","-Before the existing late_eval processing (lines 1879-1919), partition late_eval traces into:","-- `newly_late`: traces where `self.zc_monomial_cache[trace_idx].is_none()` → process with GPU (first time)","-- `cached_late`: traces where cache exists → process with CPU","+### Step 6: Replace per-trace loop with batched dispatch"," ","-For `cached_late` zerocheck traces:","-1. D2H `self.lambda_combinations[air_idx]` for each unique air_idx among cached traces (small: ~20-50 EF values per AIR, ~30-50 distinct AIRs)","-2. For each trace: `tilde_eval = sum_m(lambda_comb[m] * cached_product[m])` — CPU dot product","-3. Store in `self.zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, in `fold_ple_evals`"," ","-For `cached_late` logup traces:","-1. D2H `self.logup_combinations[trace_idx]` for each cached trace (small: ~5-10 EF values)","-2. For each trace: compute `(p, q) = sum_i(logup_comb[i] * cached_frac[i])` — CPU dot product","-3. Store in `self.logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`","+Replace lines 714-742 (the per-trace loop) with:"," ","-For `newly_late` traces: run existing GPU kernels (unchanged).","+1. **Build descriptors** (CPU): Iterate traces, compute `new_height`, `dst_offset`, create `FoldPleDesc` per trace."," ","-### Step 4: Skip the `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` for dead traces (optional follow-up)","+2. **Build BlockCtx array** (CPU): For each descriptor, compute the grid that the per-trace kernel would have used: `grid_x = ceil(new_height / chunks_per_block)`, `grid_y = trace_width`. Create one `FoldPleBlockCtx` per block."," ","-The `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` currently runs for ALL traces including dead ones (55ms total). We could add a `skip_mask` to the kernel to avoid processing height-1 traces whose results won't be used by GPU kernels. This is a follow-up optimization, not part of the initial implementation.","+3. **Upload** descriptors and BlockCtx arrays to device (`to_device()`)."," ","+4. **Single kernel launch** with `total_blocks = sum of all per-trace block counts`.","+","+5. **Fallback**: If `traces.len() < 10` (e.g., APC 0 with few traces), use the existing per-trace loop to avoid descriptor overhead.","+"," ## Invariants"," ","-1. **Correctness**: `zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]` and `logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]` must have the same values whether computed by GPU or CPU path. The CPU path produces `sum(lambda_comb[m] * cached_product[m])` which is algebraically identical to the GPU monomial kernel output.","+1. **Output equivalence**: Each trace's folded output in `folded_evals` must be identical to the per-trace kernel output. The batched kernel uses the exact same computation per block."," ","-2. **First-round handling**: On round == n_lift + 1, the trace uses the GPU kernel (to populate the cache). The `compute_batch_s_poly` function multiplies tilde_evals by `eq_r_acc` / `eq_sharp_r_acc` only on this round. This logic is UNCHANGED — it reads from tilde_evals regardless of how they were computed.","+2. **Destination non-overlap**: Each trace writes to a distinct region of `folded_evals` (offset by `dst_offset`). The batched kernel preserves this since each descriptor has its own `dst` pointer."," ","-3. **Fold no-op for height-1**: After round == n_lift, the fold kernel produces a height-1 matrix. In subsequent rounds, the fold identifies this trace as non-foldable and skips it. The matrix data persists unchanged.","+3. **omega_skip_pows and inv_lagrange_denoms**: Shared across all traces (same for the entire fold_ple_evals call). Passed as kernel parameters, not per-descriptor."," ","-4. **lambda_combinations freshness**: The D2H of lambda_combinations must happen AFTER the `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` has completed for the current round. Since `fold_mle_evals` (which calls precompute) runs before `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`, this ordering is guaranteed.","+4. **Block size consistency**: The batched kernel uses the same block size as the per-trace kernel (`max(256, skip_domain)`). Shared memory allocation is the same."," ","-5. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0, most traces have large n_lift (close to max_n_lift), so few traces enter late_eval in early rounds. The CPU path handles a small number of traces, adding negligible overhead.","+5. **APC 0 safety**: The fallback path ensures APC 0 (few traces, below threshold) is unaffected."," "," ## Measurement Plan"," ","-1. Run `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}","-2. Compare MLE Rounds metric: expect ~25-35ms reduction at APC 300 (161ms → 126-136ms)","-3. Compare STARK excl trace: expect ~25-35ms reduction at APC 300 (1129ms → 1094-1104ms)","-4. Verify APC 0 shows no regression (expect <5ms change)","-5. Profile with nsight: verify `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` and `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel` instance counts decrease significantly (from 178+43 to ~30-50, only newly-late traces)","+1. Run benchmarks for APC {0, 100, 300} using `run_pairing.sh`.","+2. Compare Stacked Reduction metric: expect ~8-12ms reduction at APC 300 (75ms → 63-67ms).","+3. Compare STARK excl trace: expect ~8-12ms reduction at APC 300 (1129ms → 1117-1121ms).","+4. Verify APC 0 shows no regression (fallback path active).","+5. nsight: verify `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel` instance count drops from ~797 to ~2-4 (one batched launch per commit group per segment)."," "," ## Rollback Criteria"," ","-- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 < 15ms (below half the expected 30ms)","-- APC 0 regression > 10ms on STARK excl trace","-- Correctness failure (proof verification fails at any APC config)","+- Stacked Reduction improvement at APC 300 < 5ms","+- APC 0 regression > 5ms on Stacked Reduction","+- Correctness failure at any APC configuration"]}],"originalFile":"# Plan: Cache Monomial Products for Dead Traces in MLE Rounds\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate GPU monomial evaluation kernel launches for \"dead\" (height-1) traces in MLE sumcheck rounds by caching per-monomial matrix-value products once and recomputing the weighted sum on CPU each round. At APC 300, this targets ~35-41ms of GPU time across `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` (17ms, 178 instances), `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel` (18ms, 43 instances), and `logup_monomial_kernel` (6ms, 356 instances).\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### MLE round flow (`LogupZerocheckGpu::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`, `mod.rs:1602`)\n\nEach MLE round (14 rounds × 2 segments at APC 300):\n\n1. **Categorize traces** (lines 1639-1875): Iterate all traces. If `round > n_lift` → `late_eval` (height-1). If `round <= n_lift` → `early_eval` (height > 1).\n\n2. **Late logup evaluation** (lines 1880-1900): Build `LogupMonomialBatch` from late traces with interactions → launch `logup_monomial_kernel` on GPU → D2H results → store in `self.logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`.\n\n3. **Late zerocheck evaluation** (lines 1901-1919): Build `ZerocheckMonomialBatch` from late traces with constraints → launch `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` on GPU → D2H results → store in `self.zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`.\n\n4. **Early trace evaluation** (lines 1922-2005): Batched logup + zerocheck via GPU kernels for height > 1 traces.\n\n5. **Combine into sumcheck polynomial** (`compute_batch_s_poly`, lines 2011-2085): For late traces (`round > n_lift`), their contribution is a constant `sp_tail += mu_pows * tilde_eval`. On the first late round (`round == n_lift + 1`), tilde_evals are multiplied by `eq_r_acc` / `eq_sharp_r_acc`.\n\n### Key observation\n\nFor height-1 traces in `late_eval`, the folded matrix is 1×width. The matrix values **do not change** between rounds (fold is a no-op at height 1). Only the `lambda_combinations` (from `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel`) and `lambda_combinations` (zerocheck) change per round.\n\nThe monomial kernel computes: `result = sum_m( lambda_comb[m] * product_j( matrix[j]^exp[m][j] ) )`\n\nThe inner products `product_j(matrix[j]^exp[m][j])` are **constant across rounds** for height-1 traces. Only `lambda_comb[m]` changes.\n\n### Where the GPU work happens\n\n- `LogupMonomialBatch::evaluate()` → launches `logup_monomial_kernel` on GPU, reads matrix values from device, evaluates interaction polynomial. File: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/monomial.rs`\n- `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::evaluate()` → launches `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` / `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel` on GPU, reads matrix values and lambda_combs from device. File: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/monomial.rs`\n\n## Changes\n\n### Step 1: Add cached monomial product storage to `LogupZerocheckGpu`\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nAdd new fields to the `LogupZerocheckGpu` struct:\n\n```rust\n/// Cached per-monomial products for height-1 traces (zerocheck).\n/// Indexed by trace_idx. None = trace not yet dead.\n/// Each inner Vec has one EF per monomial.\nzc_monomial_cache: Vec<Option<Vec<EF>>>,\n\n/// Cached per-interaction evaluation intermediates for height-1 traces (logup).\n/// Stores (p_eval, q_eval) at the frozen matrix values, before lambda_comb weighting.\n/// None = trace not yet dead.\nlogup_frozen_eval: Vec<Option<(EF, EF)>>,\n```\n\nInitialize both as `vec![None; num_traces]` in the constructor.\n\n### Step 2: Cache monomial products when trace first becomes height-1\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, in `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`\n\nFor traces entering `late_eval` for the FIRST time (identified by `round == n_lift + 1` AND `self.zc_monomial_cache[trace_idx].is_none()`):\n\n**Zerocheck path:**\n1. The existing GPU monomial kernel runs this one time (round == n_lift + 1) — this is the initial evaluation.\n2. After D2H of the result, additionally D2H the matrix row values (1 × width, from `self.mat_evals_per_trace[trace_idx]` which is at height=1 after fold).\n3. On CPU, compute the per-monomial products:\n   ```\n   for each monomial m in dag.monomials:\n       product = EF::ONE\n       for each (col_idx, exponent) in m.factors:\n           product *= matrix_values[col_idx].pow(exponent)\n       cache[m] = product\n   ```\n4. Store in `self.zc_monomial_cache[trace_idx] = Some(products)`.\n\n**Logup path:**\n1. Similarly, let the GPU kernel run for the first round.\n2. Cache `self.logup_frozen_eval[trace_idx] = Some((p_result, q_result))` from the GPU output.\n3. Since logup evaluations also depend on lambda_comb which changes, we need to cache the interaction intermediate: the fractional evaluation `(p, q)` from the matrix values alone.\n\nActually, for logup, the evaluation is:\n```\nFor each interaction: num/denom = product over fields of (alpha * value + beta)\nFrac = sum over interactions of weight * num/denom\n```\nThe `(alpha, beta)` are fixed challenges. The `value` fields read from the matrix. Since the matrix is frozen, the entire `Frac(p, q)` is frozen.\n\nBut the `logup_monomial_kernel` computes: `result = sum over interactions of (logup_comb[i] * interaction_eval[i])`, where `logup_comb[i]` changes per round.\n\nSo I need to cache the per-interaction evaluations (not the final sum). Each trace has a small number of interactions (1-10 typically).\n\nRevised logup caching:\n1. D2H the matrix row for the trace\n2. On CPU, evaluate each interaction's numerator and denominator from the matrix values\n3. Cache `Vec<Frac<EF>>` (one per interaction per trace)\n4. Each subsequent round: dot product of cached fracs with logup_comb → final `(p, q)`\n\n### Step 3: Use cached values in subsequent rounds\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, in `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`\n\nBefore the existing late_eval processing (lines 1879-1919), partition late_eval traces into:\n- `newly_late`: traces where `self.zc_monomial_cache[trace_idx].is_none()` → process with GPU (first time)\n- `cached_late`: traces where cache exists → process with CPU\n\nFor `cached_late` zerocheck traces:\n1. D2H `self.lambda_combinations[air_idx]` for each unique air_idx among cached traces (small: ~20-50 EF values per AIR, ~30-50 distinct AIRs)\n2. For each trace: `tilde_eval = sum_m(lambda_comb[m] * cached_product[m])` — CPU dot product\n3. Store in `self.zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`\n\nFor `cached_late` logup traces:\n1. D2H `self.logup_combinations[trace_idx]` for each cached trace (small: ~5-10 EF values)\n2. For each trace: compute `(p, q) = sum_i(logup_comb[i] * cached_frac[i])` — CPU dot product\n3. Store in `self.logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]`\n\nFor `newly_late` traces: run existing GPU kernels (unchanged).\n\n### Step 4: Skip the `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` for dead traces (optional follow-up)\n\nThe `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` currently runs for ALL traces including dead ones (55ms total). We could add a `skip_mask` to the kernel to avoid processing height-1 traces whose results won't be used by GPU kernels. This is a follow-up optimization, not part of the initial implementation.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: `zerocheck_tilde_evals[trace_idx]` and `logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx]` must have the same values whether computed by GPU or CPU path. The CPU path produces `sum(lambda_comb[m] * cached_product[m])` which is algebraically identical to the GPU monomial kernel output.\n\n2. **First-round handling**: On round == n_lift + 1, the trace uses the GPU kernel (to populate the cache). The `compute_batch_s_poly` function multiplies tilde_evals by `eq_r_acc` / `eq_sharp_r_acc` only on this round. This logic is UNCHANGED — it reads from tilde_evals regardless of how they were computed.\n\n3. **Fold no-op for height-1**: After round == n_lift, the fold kernel produces a height-1 matrix. In subsequent rounds, the fold identifies this trace as non-foldable and skips it. The matrix data persists unchanged.\n\n4. **lambda_combinations freshness**: The D2H of lambda_combinations must happen AFTER the `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` has completed for the current round. Since `fold_mle_evals` (which calls precompute) runs before `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`, this ordering is guaranteed.\n\n5. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0, most traces have large n_lift (close to max_n_lift), so few traces enter late_eval in early rounds. The CPU path handles a small number of traces, adding negligible overhead.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Run `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}\n2. Compare MLE Rounds metric: expect ~25-35ms reduction at APC 300 (161ms → 126-136ms)\n3. Compare STARK excl trace: expect ~25-35ms reduction at APC 300 (1129ms → 1094-1104ms)\n4. Verify APC 0 shows no regression (expect <5ms change)\n5. Profile with nsight: verify `zerocheck_monomial_kernel` and `zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel` instance counts decrease significantly (from 178+43 to ~30-50, only newly-late traces)\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 < 15ms (below half the expected 30ms)\n- APC 0 regression > 10ms on STARK excl trace\n- Correctness failure (proof verification fails at any APC config)\n"}}
