{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W6cvZwS94GjGqtMAq7a6vR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batch fold_ple Kernel Launches via Descriptor Array\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReplace the per-trace sequential loop of `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` GPU kernel launches in `fold_ple_evals` (stacked_reduction.rs:714-742) with a single batched descriptor-array kernel launch. At APC 300 with ~600-800 traces across 2 segments, this eliminates ~790 individual kernel launches and enables concurrent SM utilization across traces.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Rust side: `StackedReductionGpu::fold_ple_evals` (`stacked_reduction.rs:688-788`)\n10\t\n11\tAfter Round 0, this function folds PLE evaluations for each unstacked trace:\n12\t\n13\t```rust\n14\tfor stacked in &self.stacked_per_commit {           // ~2-3 commit groups\n15\t    let folded_evals = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(num_x * stacked_width);\n16\t    folded_evals.fill_zero()?;\n17\t    let mut dst_offset = 0;\n18\t    for trace in &stacked.traces {                   // ~300 traces at APC 300\n19\t        stacked_reduction_fold_ple(                  // <-- One kernel launch per trace\n20\t            trace.buffer().as_ptr(), dst, ...,\n21\t            trace.height(), trace.width(), l_skip,\n22\t        )?;\n23\t        dst_offset += new_height * trace.width();\n24\t    }\n25\t    self.q_evals.push(folded_evals);\n26\t}\n27\t```\n28\t\n29\tEach `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` call launches `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` with grid `(ceil(new_height / chunks_per_block), trace_width)` and block `(max(256, skip_domain))`.\n30\t\n31\t### CUDA kernel: `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` (`stacked_reduction.cu:175-229`)\n32\t\n33\tGrid: 2D — `blockIdx.x` selects row chunk, `blockIdx.y` selects column.\n34\tPer-thread: barycentric interpolation of `skip_domain` source values into one output FpExt value.\n35\t\n36\tParameters per trace: `src` (source trace pointer), `dst` (output pointer within folded_evals buffer), `trace_height`, `new_height`, `skip_domain`.\n37\t\n38\t### Why it's slow\n39\t\n40\tnsight profiling shows `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel<false>` (same kernel, different instantiation): **797 instances, 9.5ms total, avg 12μs per instance**.\n41\t\n42\tTwo costs:\n43\t1. **Kernel launch overhead**: ~5μs × 797 = ~4ms. Each launch goes through the CUDA driver.\n44\t2. **Sequential SM utilization**: Each per-trace kernel runs alone on the GPU. Small traces (low width × low height) use few blocks, leaving most of the 128 SMs idle. The next trace's kernel only starts after the previous completes.\n45\t\n46\tWith a batched kernel, all traces' blocks launch simultaneously. The GPU scheduler distributes them across SMs concurrently, converting sum-of-times into max-of-times.\n47\t\n48\t## Changes\n49\t\n50\t### Step 1: Define descriptor struct\n51\t\n52\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n53\t\n54\tAdd a `FoldPleDesc` descriptor:\n55\t\n56\t```c\n57\tstruct FoldPleDesc {\n58\t    const Fp *src;          // source trace pointer\n59\t    FpExt *dst;             // destination within folded_evals buffer\n60\t    uint32_t trace_height;\n61\t    uint32_t new_height;\n62\t    uint32_t trace_width;\n63\t};\n64\t```\n65\t\n66\t### Step 2: Add BlockCtx mapping struct\n67\t\n68\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n69\t\n70\tAdd a `FoldPleBlockCtx` that maps each CUDA block to a (descriptor_index, local_block_x, column_idx):\n71\t\n72\t```c\n73\tstruct FoldPleBlockCtx {\n74\t    uint32_t desc_idx;      // index into FoldPleDesc array\n75\t    uint32_t local_block_x; // row chunk index within this trace\n76\t    uint32_t col_idx;       // column index within this trace\n77\t};\n78\t```\n79\t\n80\t### Step 3: Implement batched kernel\n81\t\n82\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n83\t\n84\tAdd `batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel`:\n85\t\n86\t```c\n87\t__global__ void batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel(\n88\t    const FoldPleBlockCtx *block_ctx,\n89\t    const FoldPleDesc *descs,\n90\t    const Fp *omega_skip_pows,\n91\t    const FpExt *inv_lagrange_denoms,\n92\t    uint32_t skip_domain\n93\t) {\n94\t    auto ctx = block_ctx[blockIdx.x];\n95\t    auto desc = descs[ctx.desc_idx];\n96\t    \n97\t    uint32_t col_idx = ctx.col_idx;\n98\t    uint32_t chunks_per_block = blockDim.x / skip_domain;\n99\t    uint32_t chunk_in_block = threadIdx.x / skip_domain;\n100\t    uint32_t tid_in_chunk = threadIdx.x % skip_domain;\n101\t    uint32_t row_idx = ctx.local_block_x * chunks_per_block + chunk_in_block;\n102\t    \n103\t    // Same body as stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel, but reading from desc\n104\t    bool const active_chunk = (row_idx < desc.new_height);\n105\t    // ... (barycentric interpolation using desc.src, desc.trace_height, desc.trace_width)\n106\t    // Output to desc.dst at appropriate offset\n107\t}\n108\t```\n109\t\n110\tThe block body is identical to the existing `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` but reads `src`, `dst`, `trace_height`, `new_height` from the descriptor instead of kernel parameters.\n111\t\n112\t### Step 4: Add C launcher function\n113\t\n114\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`\n115\t\n116\t```c\n117\textern \"C\" int _batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple(\n118\t    const FoldPleBlockCtx *block_ctx,\n119\t    const FoldPleDesc *descs,\n120\t    uint32_t total_blocks,\n121\t    const Fp *omega_skip_pows,\n122\t    const FpExt *inv_lagrange_denoms,\n123\t    uint32_t l_skip\n124\t) {\n125\t    uint32_t skip_domain = 1u << l_skip;\n126\t    uint32_t block_size = std::max(256u, skip_domain);\n127\t    uint32_t total_warps = block_size / WARP_SIZE;\n128\t    size_t smem_bytes = (skip_domain > WARP_SIZE) ? total_warps * sizeof(FpExt) : 0;\n129\t    \n130\t    batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel<<<total_blocks, block_size, smem_bytes>>>(\n131\t        block_ctx, descs, omega_skip_pows, inv_lagrange_denoms, skip_domain\n132\t    );\n133\t    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n134\t}\n135\t```\n136\t\n137\t### Step 5: Add Rust FFI bindings\n138\t\n139\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`\n140\t\n141\tAdd `#[repr(C)]` structs for `FoldPleDesc` and `FoldPleBlockCtx`, plus the extern \"C\" declaration and safe wrapper:\n142\t\n143\t```rust\n144\t#[repr(C)]\n145\tpub struct FoldPleDesc {\n146\t    pub src: *const F,\n147\t    pub dst: *mut EF,\n148\t    pub trace_height: u32,\n149\t    pub new_height: u32,\n150\t    pub trace_width: u32,\n151\t}\n152\t\n153\t#[repr(C)]\n154\tpub struct FoldPleBlockCtx {\n155\t    pub desc_idx: u32,\n156\t    pub local_block_x: u32,\n157\t    pub col_idx: u32,\n158\t}\n159\t```\n160\t\n161\t### Step 6: Replace per-trace loop with batched dispatch\n162\t\n163\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, in `fold_ple_evals`\n164\t\n165\tReplace lines 714-742 (the per-trace loop) with:\n166\t\n167\t1. **Build descriptors** (CPU): Iterate traces, compute `new_height`, `dst_offset`, create `FoldPleDesc` per trace.\n168\t\n169\t2. **Build BlockCtx array** (CPU): For each descriptor, compute the grid that the per-trace kernel would have used: `grid_x = ceil(new_height / chunks_per_block)`, `grid_y = trace_width`. Create one `FoldPleBlockCtx` per block.\n170\t\n171\t3. **Upload** descriptors and BlockCtx arrays to device (`to_device()`).\n172\t\n173\t4. **Single kernel launch** with `total_blocks = sum of all per-trace block counts`.\n174\t\n175\t5. **Fallback**: If `traces.len() < 10` (e.g., APC 0 with few traces), use the existing per-trace loop to avoid descriptor overhead.\n176\t\n177\t## Invariants\n178\t\n179\t1. **Output equivalence**: Each trace's folded output in `folded_evals` must be identical to the per-trace kernel output. The batched kernel uses the exact same computation per block.\n180\t\n181\t2. **Destination non-overlap**: Each trace writes to a distinct region of `folded_evals` (offset by `dst_offset`). The batched kernel preserves this since each descriptor has its own `dst` pointer.\n182\t\n183\t3. **omega_skip_pows and inv_lagrange_denoms**: Shared across all traces (same for the entire fold_ple_evals call). Passed as kernel parameters, not per-descriptor.\n184\t\n185\t4. **Block size consistency**: The batched kernel uses the same block size as the per-trace kernel (`max(256, skip_domain)`). Shared memory allocation is the same.\n186\t\n187\t5. **APC 0 safety**: The fallback path ensures APC 0 (few traces, below threshold) is unaffected.\n188\t\n189\t## Measurement Plan\n190\t\n191\t1. Run benchmarks for APC {0, 100, 300} using `run_pairing.sh`.\n192\t2. Compare Stacked Reduction metric: expect ~8-12ms reduction at APC 300 (75ms → 63-67ms).\n193\t3. Compare STARK excl trace: expect ~8-12ms reduction at APC 300 (1129ms → 1117-1121ms).\n194\t4. Verify APC 0 shows no regression (fallback path active).\n195\t5. nsight: verify `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel` instance count drops from ~797 to ~2-4 (one batched launch per commit group per segment).\n196\t\n197\t## Rollback Criteria\n198\t\n199\t- Stacked Reduction improvement at APC 300 < 5ms\n200\t- APC 0 regression > 5ms on Stacked Reduction\n201\t- Correctness failure at any APC configuration\n202\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0192Qvd465i9XpeWnmukTiDV","session_id":"97df75af-c1b3-4850-8566-f6b18608e3d5","uuid":"c0b0a3d3-c4fd-485e-ba62-ecda0d06a631","timestamp":"2026-04-16T00:13:02.165Z"}
